[38;5;252m[1m## Bug found and fixed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mc2Dot[0m returned the wrong NaN payload. The C's gcc output is:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mmulss %xmm0,%xmm1     # dst = a.x, src = b.x
mulss %xmm2,%xmm0     # dst = b.y, src = a.y   <- operands swapped
addss %xmm1,%xmm0     # dst = y product
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
On x86 SSE the [3mdestination[23m operand's NaN wins, and [38;5;10m0.0 * INFINITY[0m yields the negative indefinite [38;5;10m0xFFC00000[0m. Because gcc makes the y-product the add destination and LLVM made the x-product the destination, [38;5;10mc2Dot((0,0), (inf, NaN))[0m gave [38;5;10m0x7FC00000[0m from C and [38;5;10m0xFFC00000[0m from Rust.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Rust does not specify which operand of [38;5;10m+[0m becomes the destination register, so instead of writing the expression backwards and hoping LLVM cooperates, I added explicit [38;5;10mmulss[0m/[38;5;10maddss[0m/[38;5;10msubss[0m/[38;5;10mdivss[0m helpers in [38;5;10msrc/lib.rs[0m that spell out the NaN selection (destination first, then source, quieting SNaNs). The same fix applies to the two [38;5;10mA.r + B.r[0m sites in [38;5;10mc2CircletoCircle[0m and [38;5;10mc2CircletoCapsule[0m, which gcc also emits with [38;5;10mB.r[0m as the destination. Confirmed against the C disassembly of every arithmetic site.[0m[0m
